# Bulk artifacts kept out of git history: path, sha256, byte size

These are the Slot-B working bytes that the repository policy classifies as scratch
rather than as findings. They are deliberately not in the tree any more — the
conclusions drawn from them live in the numbered `slot-b-investigation/*.md` reports and
in `untouched-b-early-boot-forensics/`, `boot-chain-compare/` and
`slot-b-first-boot-hang/` findings docs, not in the raw dumps themselves.

They stay on disk beside this manifest, and this manifest is the recovery pointer:
`sha256sum -c`-style verification still works against the path column, so an untracked
dump can be re-pulled from the device or re-unpacked from a pinned image and checked
byte-for-byte against the exact bytes a conclusion was based on.

Two classes are listed:

- `raw-machine-code` — partition images pulled off the device, unpacked boot/vendor_boot
  containers and their extracted ramdisk, dtb and bootconfig members, cpio archives and
  lz4 streams. Re-derivable from the hash-pinned sources in `01-*.txt` /
  `05-device-dump-manifest.txt` (the `device-dumps/*.img` rows deliberately duplicate that
  manifest so this file is self-contained).
- `bulk-scan` — whole-volume `rg`/`grep` sweeps whose interesting lines are already quoted
  in the findings docs. Re-running the same search over the same pinned bytes reproduces
  them; the dumps themselves reach hundreds of MB because matched lines carry whole binary
  files as context.

Each row was hashed from the on-disk bytes at untracking time; the files themselves were
never rewritten or moved. To re-verify every row still matches the bytes a conclusion was
based on, run from this directory:

    sed -n '/^[0-9a-f]\{64\}  /p' BULK-ARTIFACTS.sha256 | awk '{print $1"  "$2}' | sha256sum -c -

